---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Questions from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Questions.astro";
import { u } from '../lib/url.js';
import data from '../data/open-questions.json';

/* 22 September 2026. The ten questions were a JavaScript array inside this
   file — a five-tuple each, with the link target looked up from a second map
   below it. They are src/data/open-questions.json now, extracted by RUNNING
   the array rather than retyping it, and the page draws the kit's Questions,
   which six other archives already use.

   The component's own header says the five archives keeping their questions
   as prose "cannot use this until somebody extracts them, and that
   extraction is research work belonging to each archive". That was true of
   prose and was never true here: these were already structured — number,
   question, what is known, what would settle it, where it stands — and only
   the storage was wrong. Nothing was reworded, reordered or graded.

   NO `state` IS INVENTED. The other archives grade a question open, blocked
   or answered, and this archive never has; the component draws no filter bar
   when there is nothing to filter. Question 2 says ANSWERED SO FAR AS
   RECORDS CAN in its own first line, which is a sentence and not a grade,
   and turning it into one would be this archive's call. */
const rows = data.rows;
---
<Base title="Open questions"
  subtitle="Ten things this archive cannot answer yet, in the order they are worth attacking"
  lede="An archive is worth what its unanswered questions are worth. These are written so that anyone — a cousin, a stranger, an archivist — can pick one up and work it without asking permission.">

  <Questions rows={rows} u={u} />

  <h2>Requests, drafted</h2>
  <p class="small muted">
    Written out in full in <code>requests/</code> — the Porto one in Portuguese and English, ready to send.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Conservatória do Registo Civil do Porto</strong> — death and burial of Ivone de Figueiredo, b. 4 March 1924 Lourenço Marques.</li>
    <li><strong>Arquivo Histórico de Moçambique</strong>, Maputo, and the <strong>Arquivo Histórico Ultramarino</strong>, Lisbon — Lourenço Marques civil registration, 1924 and c. 1941.</li>
    <li><strong>Landesarchiv Berlin</strong> — Schöneberg marriage 5 January 1924; the divorce file; Kurt's 1912 birth entry; Gertrud Leibke's death, 27 February 1943.</li>
    <li><strong>Arolsen Archives</strong> — Jacob, Samuel, Georg, Oskar, Elli and Werner Leibholz.</li>
    <li><strong>Master of the Supreme Court, Pretoria</strong> — estate file 7584/1969, Kurt Luwinski.</li>
    <li><strong>National Archives of South Africa</strong> — the 1948 and 1951 divorce actions, and the Germiston marriage register.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
